Here's what the plan gives you: 24 study days, 4 rest days, and 7 past paper days. You start with Natural Sciences — matter and materials, energy and change, life and living — 60 minutes each. Math gets its own blocks too, including 150 minutes on finance and 120 on statistics since those are your weak spots. The last day, 2026-12-18, is a light 30-minute review of finance. Past papers come later, after you've learned the topics, so you actually know what you're testing. Want me to walk you through the first week?

Good question. We do start with algebra because it's the foundation for sequences and functions. The schedule gives your weak topics the most time: sequences and finance get 540 minutes each, stats gets 480. Everything else gets 240-300. We mix them in so you don't burn out on one topic. First block is 60 min on algebra today, then sequences tomorrow. That's time-boxing — one hour, focused, then done.

